<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<subsection xml:id="assess_intro">
	<title>Introduction to Ungrading</title>
        <p>
	As mentioned earlier, substantial evidence shows that grades and grading are inherently antithetical to learning. <em>Ungrading</em> shifts the goal of a course from achievement to learning. 
	</p>
        <p>
			It is also important to note that the content of this course focuses on developing conceptual and procedural fluency with a set of concepts and tools as well as developing a <q>mathematical mindset</q>. Thus, it makes sense to have learning targets that students need to satisfy in order to consider the course successful.
	</p>
<p>
	Ultimately, though, this is your course. It is you who are the learner, and so you are in the best position to determine what will be of the most interest, what will be the most engaging, and, beyond the basics, what will be of the most value to you. More than that, <em>you</em> are the best person to determine how much you have learned. 
	 Therefore, you will be an active participant in determining the direction of the course and in your evaluation.
</p>
<p>
	To put things bluntly, I will determine, based on a set of learning targets and a body of evidence that you present to me, whether you have done the work necessary to pass the class. Beyond that, <em>you</em> will determine your own grade, based on the evidence you present that you have met your own goals, as outlined in 
<xref ref="portfolio" text='title' />, <xref ref="portfolio" text="title" />.
</p>
</subsection>
